# K1 / B002 — Run 2 replica annotation (blind). Paths relative to output/template/.

# ---------------- constraints first defined in this batch ----------------
C X12 EX - | MUST | Every instruction artifact is classified in the order Capability -> Kind -> Scope: capability-owned artifacts declare Capability: <slot>/<implementation> (others Capability: —); Kind (OP, DEC, STD, KNW, WF, ENF, IN) decides mechanism and location; Scope (common, backend, backend-tests, frontend, delivery, repo/meta) decides the rule directory and paths:. | Authoring or changing instruction-system artifacts | none stated | Instruction-system classification scheme of the K1 design; no oracle policy dictates how instruction artifacts are classified, headed or located. | A new rule or skill must be placed and headed according to its Capability/Kind/Scope classification; misplacement is a structural error.
C X13 EX - | MUST | Instruction dependency direction is WF -> KNW -> STD -> DEC -> OP and ENF -> STD/DEC; each ENF artifact declares what it conforms to on a Conforms: line directly after its header. | Instruction-system artifacts | Skill and agent references are routing pointers, not dependencies. | Internal dependency discipline of the instruction system, not derived from any V2 policy. | A standard may not depend on a workflow; an enforcement script must name the standard/decision it conforms to.
C X14 EX - | MUST | Single owner per topic: each rule/topic lives in the artifact recorded as its owner in the map (Owns / Extend here when / Where does a topic live), new content is added at its owner, and the map is updated whenever an artifact is added, moved, or removed. | Instruction-system maintenance | none stated | Research construction principle (one normative owner) realised as an instruction-maintenance rule; not one of the 196 V2 policies. | Adding a duplicate rule in a second file or adding an artifact without updating MAP.md is non-conformant (checked by the structure checker).
C X15 EX GOV-017 | MUST (Reserved for contracts) | Changes to the instruction system go through the user-invoked template-maintenance workflow, and changing a slot contract is a Reserved decision (rare). | Instruction-system and capability-contract changes | none stated | GOV-017 makes Reserved changes approval-gated but does not make the template's own slot contracts Reserved or route instruction changes through a user-invoked maintenance workflow. | Editing a slot CONTRACT.md requires explicit approval; template changes are not made through ordinary task workflows.
C R285 OL GOV-017,GOV-001,GOV-003 | MUST NOT (silently) | Product, feature and task inputs may request a decision change but must not silently override engineering policy or recorded decisions; requested decision changes go through decision control (an explicit user instruction counts as approval). | Conflicts between product/task inputs and policy or recorded decisions | Requirements already in the approved specification are Decided, not Reserved.
C R10 OL GOV-017,ARC-020,DEP-002 | MUST (Reserved) | Capability-selection changes and other decision-register changes are Reserved decisions handled through the architecture-decision workflow. | Changing selected implementations, approved stack or other register entries | none stated
C R11 OL API-013 | MUST NOT | Do not introduce API versioning merely because an API exists; add it only when published compatibility or coexistence of client generations creates a concrete need. | backend API (rule loads for backend/**) | none stated
C X17 EX API-013,DEP-017,ARC-020 | MUST (Reserved) | Adopting API versioning is a Reserved decision (explicit approval required, per CLAUDE.md). | Introducing API versioning in the backend | none stated | API-013 only forbids versioning without a concrete need and DEP-017 makes the package conditional; neither requires explicit approval to adopt versioning once a concrete need exists, and ARC-020's approval list does not include API versioning. | Even with a real compatibility need, the agent must obtain explicit approval before adding versioning.
C R12 OL API-014 | MUST | Once an API version is relied on by external clients it must not change incompatibly in silence; a breaking contract change requires a new version or another explicitly approved compatibility strategy. | published backend APIs | none stated
C R13 OL API-015 | DEFAULT | When explicit versioning is required, use one strategy consistently; URL-segment versioning unless project requirements justify another. | backend API versioning | project requirements may justify another strategy
C R14 OL ARC-026 | MUST NOT | Do not create separate Domain or persistence models merely because the external API has multiple versions; isolate version-specific transport contracts at the Api boundary when practical. | versioned backend APIs | none stated
C R15 OL API-016 | MUST | Swagger/OpenAPI metadata must describe the API that actually runs (routes, important response statuses, schemas, authentication behavior); never document speculative responses. | backend API documentation | none stated
C R16 OL API-017,DEP-012 | DEFAULT | Swagger UI is enabled in Development and exposed in other environments only when a deployment requirement explicitly justifies it. | backend API documentation exposure | explicit deployment requirement
C R17 OL API-001 | MUST | HTTP status codes and transport response semantics belong to the Api layer; Application and Domain must not return IActionResult or ASP.NET Core status codes. | backend | none stated
C R18 OL API-003 | MUST | Success statuses follow actual HTTP semantics (200/201/202/204): never 200 for every outcome, 201 merely because a row was inserted internally, or 202 for already-completed work. | backend HTTP endpoints | none stated
C R19 OL API-004 | DEFAULT | A successful collection query with zero matches returns 200 with an empty collection, not 404. | backend collection endpoints | none stated
C R20 OL API-005 | MUST | Use 401 for missing/failed authentication and 403 for an authenticated principal that is not permitted; returning 404 to hide a protected resource requires a deliberate, consistent security policy. | backend HTTP endpoints | deliberate consistent 404-hiding policy
C R21 OL API-006 | MUST | Use 409 for a valid request conflicting with current state and 412 when an explicit HTTP precondition such as If-Match fails. | backend HTTP endpoints | none stated
C R22 OL API-002 | DEFAULT | Use ProblemDetails for non-validation HTTP failures and ValidationProblemDetails for request validation failures; keep machine-readable error contracts stable and safe. | backend HTTP errors | none stated
C R23 OL API-007 | DEFAULT | Unexpected failures propagate to centralized IExceptionHandler handling; avoid repetitive catch/log/rethrow blocks. | backend | none stated
C R24 OL API-008 | MUST NOT | Never expose stack traces, SQL, connection strings, internal paths, secret values or sensitive exception details in public API errors. | backend public API errors | none stated
C R25 OL API-012 | MUST | Log an unexpected exception once, at the boundary that handles it; never log the same exception again across repository, Application, controller and global handler. | backend | none stated
C R26 OL API-021 | MUST NOT | Normal client-request cancellation must not be mapped to a generic 500 or logged as an unhandled application failure. | backend request handling | none stated
C R27 OL API-009 | DEFAULT | Represent common expected outcomes explicitly when useful (null, bool, a typed outcome); do not force a generic Result<T> pattern and do not use HTTP-named outcome categories in Application. | backend Application | none stated
C R28 OL API-010,DEP-010 | MUST | Use FluentValidation for explicit request/use-case validation where appropriate; validation stays at its owning boundary (transport in Api, use-case/workflow rules in Application, non-trivial invariants in Domain, external-system assumptions in Infrastructure when needed) without mechanical duplication at every layer. | backend validation | none stated
C R29 OL API-011 | MUST NOT | Do not establish business exceptions as the default architecture or throw/catch exceptions merely to jump between normal expected branches. | backend | none stated
C R30 OL ARC-001,DEP-024,ARC-012 | MUST (role map) | The Clean Architecture frame maps backend roles to projects backend/<Name>.Domain (business concepts, independent of infrastructure/transport), backend/<Name>.Application (use-case orchestration; persistence, external-service and commit-boundary abstractions), backend/<Name>.Infrastructure (implementations of Application abstractions) and backend/<Name>.Api (HTTP entry point and composition root). | backend project structure | none stated
C R31 OL ARC-001 | MUST | Dependencies point inward: Domain references no other application project; Application references Domain; Infrastructure references Application and Domain; Api references Application and may reference Infrastructure only for composition and registration; inner layers never depend on outer layers and Domain/Application never reference Infrastructure-specific types. | backend project references and types | Api -> Infrastructure only for composition/registration
C R32 OL ARC-001 | SHOULD | If a feature seems to need an outward reference, reconsider the abstraction boundary instead of adding the reference. | backend design | none stated
C R33 OL ARC-002 | MUST | Domain stays independent of the persistence technology, ASP.NET Core, HTTP and external-service implementation concerns. | backend Domain | none stated
C R34 OL ARC-003 | DEFAULT | Simple domain entities by default; straightforward CRUD/workflow rules (validation rules, immutable fields, status transitions, allowed/forbidden edits) belong in Application Services and do not by themselves justify a richer model. | backend Domain/Application | none stated
C R35 OL ARC-004 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | Move behavior into Domain only when non-trivial invariants or behavior materially benefit from domain-level encapsulation; otherwise a Rich Domain Model or DDD tactical patterns require approval. | backend Domain modeling | unless the approved specification already requires them
C R36 OL ARC-005 | MUST NOT | Do not add factories, private setters, transition methods, Aggregate Roots, Domain Events, Specifications, Domain Services, systematic Value Objects or similar DDD constructs merely to make a simple model look domain-driven. | backend Domain | none stated
C R37 OL ARC-006,GOV-013 | DEFAULT | Application Services orchestrate use cases (Domain objects, repositories, external-service abstractions, Unit of Work); a large service is a signal to reconsider responsibilities, not an automatic reason for another pattern. | backend Application | none stated
C R38 OL ARC-007 | MUST | Application defines the persistence and external-service abstractions its use cases require and keeps infrastructure implementation details out. | backend Application | none stated
C R39 OL ARC-008 | MUST NOT | Application must not depend on persistence-technology types/APIs (database context, entity sets, query or provider types) or ASP.NET Core transport types. | backend Application | none stated
C R40 OL ARC-016 | DEFAULT | IUnitOfWork is the commit boundary of an Application use case; repositories stage changes and Application Services decide when to commit. | backend Application/persistence | none stated
C R41 OL ARC-017,ARC-021 | MUST | Repositories in one use case share the same scoped Unit of Work and persistence context; repositories are injected explicitly into consumers and IUnitOfWork must not become a repository container or service locator. | backend persistence | none stated
C R42 OL ARC-012 | DEFAULT | Program.cs in Api is the application composition root and may register Infrastructure implementations. | backend Api | none stated
C R43 OL ARC-023 | DEFAULT | Registration stays near the owning layer: Application registers Application services, Infrastructure registers persistence and adapters, Api composes them (AddApplication(), AddInfrastructure(configuration)). | backend DI registration | none stated
C R44 OL ARC-029 | MUST NOT | Do not duplicate equivalent DTOs across Api and Application merely to create layer-shaped types; introduce transport-specific contracts in Api when separation provides a concrete boundary benefit. | backend contracts | concrete boundary benefit
C R45 OL ARC-010 | MUST | ASP.NET Core Controllers are the default HTTP entry point and stay thin: bind/validate input, invoke Application services, translate outcomes to HTTP responses. | backend Api | none stated
C R46 OL ARC-011 | MUST NOT | Controllers must not contain business logic, access the database context or concrete repositories, or construct infrastructure services manually. | backend Api | none stated
C R47 OL ARC-021 | DEFAULT | Constructor injection for required dependencies; avoid service-locator and property-injection patterns. | backend DI | none stated
C R48 OL ARC-022 | MUST | Dependency lifetimes must be valid; never capture a shorter-lived dependency inside a longer-lived service. | backend DI | none stated
C R49 OL ARC-024 | MUST | Cache technology stays out of Domain and infrastructure cache APIs must not leak into Application without a deliberate boundary abstraction. | backend caching | deliberate boundary abstraction
C R50 OL ARC-025 | MUST | Infrastructure translates remote HTTP responses into Application-facing abstractions; HttpResponseMessage and remote transport concerns must not leak into Application. | backend outbound HTTP | none stated
