C R73 OL PST-001 | DEFAULT | An empty Development database receives a small deterministic representative seed dataset covering the main meaningful states and relationships. | backend Development seeding | none stated
C R74 OL PST-004,TST-008 | MUST NOT | Automatic seeding runs only in Development, never automatically in Production; the development seeder is not test setup and tests own their setup. | backend seeding | none stated
C R75 OL PST-006 | MUST NOT | When representative data already exists leave it unchanged; never use the seeder for automatic synchronization, repair, upgrade, overwrite or recreation of an existing Development database (developers recreate local databases explicitly). | backend Development seeding | explicit developer recreation
C R76 OL PST-007 | MUST | Seed entities preserve current Domain invariants and valid relationships; prefer readable deterministic examples over exhaustive or random combinations. | backend Development seeding | none stated
C R77 OL RUN-001 | DEFAULT | Clear, correct code first; no specialized optimization without a concrete reason, and measurement/profiling evidence for non-trivial optimizations. | backend | none stated
C R78 OL RUN-008 | DEFAULT | Compiled queries, ValueTask, pooling, custom serialization, aggressive caching and raw SQL for performance only for a measured or clearly demonstrated need. | backend | none stated
C R79 OL RUN-002 | MUST | Use async APIs for asynchronous I/O and never block them with .Result, .Wait() or similar sync-over-async patterns. | backend | none stated
C R80 OL RUN-003 | MUST | Propagate CancellationToken through asynchronous operations when cancellation is meaningful; do not add cancellation parameters to purely synchronous code without a use case. | backend | none stated
C R81 OL RUN-004 | DEFAULT | TimeProvider for logic depending on the current clock that needs deterministic tests; do not mechanically replace explicit or stored timestamps. | backend | none stated
C R82 OL RUN-006 | DEFAULT | Filter and project in the database when practical, avoid unnecessary round trips, over-fetching and N+1, and paginate potentially large result sets. | backend data access | none stated
C R83 OL RUN-005 | MUST NOT | Never create and dispose a new HttpClient per request; use IHttpClientFactory or another appropriate long-lived client strategy. | backend outbound HTTP | none stated
C R84 OL RUN-015 | MUST | External I/O calls have a bounded execution time appropriate to the operation; avoid overlapping timeout mechanisms with unclear combined behavior. | backend outbound calls | none stated
C R85 OL RUN-016 | MUST | Retry only when repeating the operation is safe; never automatically retry state-changing operations without idempotency or another concrete safety mechanism. | backend outbound calls | idempotency or other safety mechanism
C R86 OL RUN-017 | DEFAULT | Respect Retry-After or equivalent rate-limit guidance where appropriate; never retry indefinitely or aggressively. | backend outbound calls | none stated
C R87 OL RUN-018 | MUST | Use a fallback only when its result is semantically acceptable; never hide correctness failures behind convenient default values. | backend outbound calls | none stated
C R88 OL RUN-019 | MUST NOT | Caller cancellation is not a transient failure: never retry it or convert it into repeated outbound attempts. | backend outbound calls | none stated
C R89 OL RUN-007 | DEFAULT | No caching without a clear measured or reasoned need; choose the implementation according to deployment, consistency and staleness requirements. | backend caching | none stated
C R90 OL RUN-020 | MUST | Cache keys uniquely represent the cached value including relevant user, tenant and context dimensions; no global keys for user- or tenant-specific data. | backend caching | none stated
C R91 OL RUN-023 | MUST | Every cache entry has a lifetime or another explicit invalidation strategy. | backend caching | none stated
C R92 OL RUN-021 | MUST | Invalidate/update cache entries only consistently with successful underlying mutations; the cache never becomes a second source of truth. | backend caching | none stated
C R93 OL SEC-006 | MUST | Anonymous access must be deliberate and visible; avoid redundant authorization attributes when a secure global or fallback policy already expresses the intent. | backend authorization | none stated
C R94 OL SEC-016 | MUST NOT | Do not implement custom token or password security, password hashing or reset-token cryptography when established ASP.NET Core authentication/Identity facilities cover the requirement. | backend authentication | none stated
C R95 OL SEC-017 | MUST | Bearer/OIDC authentication validates what the actual issuer/provider requires (issuer, audience where applicable, signature/signing authority, lifetime); never disable validation merely to make a token work. | backend authentication | none stated
C R96 OL SEC-018 | MUST NOT | Authentication failures must not reveal secrets or account state unnecessarily; login avoids account-enumeration leaks where relevant. | backend authentication | none stated
C R97 OL SEC-008 | MUST NOT | Do not implement custom cryptography when a well-reviewed platform mechanism is appropriate, and do not treat ASP.NET Core Data Protection as universal database encryption. | backend cryptography | none stated
C R98 OL SEC-009 | DEFAULT | CORS is the narrowest policy satisfying actual browser-client requirements (a public unauthenticated API may legitimately differ from a private browser application); never combine credentialed cross-origin requests with unrestricted origins. | backend CORS | none stated
C R99 OL SEC-015 | MUST NOT | Do not treat client-provided file metadata such as MIME type as authoritative when upload handling or security decisions depend on actual content. | backend uploads | none stated
C R100 OL TST-001,DEP-013 | DEFAULT | NUnit is the .NET automated test framework; do not introduce a second test framework without a concrete reason. | backend tests (rule loads for backend/**/*Tests/**/*) | In an established project that deliberately uses another framework, follow that convention.
C R101 OL ARC-028 | MUST NOT | Backend test projects must not have broader production-project references than their test scope requires; they follow the same inward dependency discipline as production projects. | backend test projects | none stated
C R102 OL ARC-028,DEP-013 | SHOULD (typical matrix; enforced by the architecture-style facet) | Typical test references: <Name>.Domain.Tests (optional) -> Domain; <Name>.Application.Tests -> Application, Domain as needed; <Name>.IntegrationTests -> Api (other layers exercised through the host); <Name>.Infrastructure.Tests (optional) -> Infrastructure, narrower references as required. | backend test projects | narrower references as required
C R103 OL TST-002 | DEFAULT | Choose the narrowest test level that meaningfully verifies the behavior; integration tests when framework wiring, HTTP, persistence, serialization or authentication is the subject; no fixed unit/integration ratio. | backend tests | none stated
C R104 OL TST-003 | MUST | Tests verify observable behavior, survive internal refactoring, do not test private calls or exact internal call sequences, and do not reproduce the implementation's algorithm; interaction verification only when the interaction is the specified behavior. | backend tests | interaction itself is the specified behavior
C R105 OL TST-006,DEP-013 | DEFAULT | WebApplicationFactory-based integration tests when routing, model binding, middleware, authentication/authorization, DI, repositories, the persistence technology or database persistence is what the test must prove. | backend integration tests | none stated
C R106 OL TST-007 | DEFAULT | Use the actual database provider when relational/provider behavior matters (Testcontainers optional); never claim provider fidelity from a test that runs against a different provider. | backend tests | none stated
C R107 OL TST-004 | MUST | Tests are deterministic, independent of execution order and isolated from Production and Development data, uncontrolled external systems and shared mutable state. | backend tests | none stated
C R108 OL TST-008 | MUST | Tests start from a known state and own their setup; never depend on development seed data or the order in which other tests run. | backend tests | none stated
C R109 OL TST-005 | DEFAULT | For small finite input/state spaces use explicit declarative cases instead of loops, branches, LINQ or generated case machinery; expected results are stated, not computed. | backend tests | none stated
C R110 OL TST-012 | DEFAULT | Descriptive test names communicating scenario/condition and expected behavior (e.g. MethodOrScenario_Condition_ExpectedBehavior) unless the project has an established convention. | backend tests | established project convention
C R111 OL TST-011 | MUST NOT | Do not generate low-value tests merely to increase a coverage percentage. | backend tests | none stated
C R112 OL DEP-001,GOV-013 | MUST | Add a dependency only for a concrete current requirement; never install packages for hypothetical future use; an approved technology is not a reason to install it by default. | all ecosystems (rule loads for **/*) | none stated
C R113 OL DEP-003,DEP-004,DEP-005 | MUST | Every NuGet or npm addition or update runs the add-dependency workflow. | dependency changes | none stated
C R114 OL SEC-001 | MUST NOT | Never hardcode or commit real secrets, credentials, private keys, access/refresh tokens or production connection strings, including in example configuration files. | all code and configuration | none stated
C R115 OL SEC-002 | DEFAULT | A local secret mechanism (e.g. dotnet user-secrets) for development and environment-specific secret injection or a managed secret store for deployed environments; do not assume a cloud provider the project has not chosen. | secrets handling | none stated
C R116 OL SEC-003 | MUST | Data crossing the trusted boundary (HTTP requests, uploads, external responses, message payloads, user-provided identifiers and filters) is untrusted; validate it at the owning layer when assumptions matter. | all code | none stated
C R117 OL SEC-005 | MUST | Authorization is enforced on the server; frontend route guards, hidden/disabled UI actions and client checks are UX, not a security boundary. | all code | none stated
C R118 OL SEC-007 | MUST | Production traffic carrying credentials or sensitive data uses TLS; respect actual hosting topology (TLS may terminate at app, proxy, ingress, gateway or platform), so app-level HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection is not required where deployment already provides a secure transport boundary and redirection is not desired. | production transport | secure transport boundary provided by deployment
C R119 OL SEC-007 | MUST NOT | Never disable certificate validation to make an integration work. | outbound/inbound TLS | none stated
C R120 OL SEC-010 | MUST NOT | Never log passwords, access/refresh tokens, private keys, authentication secrets or full payment credentials; avoid PII unless a concrete operational requirement exists and policy permits; lower log levels do not make sensitive data safe; prefer stable technical identifiers. | all logging | concrete permitted operational need for PII
C R121 OL RUN-014 | MUST NOT | Do not serialize arbitrary request bodies, domain objects or sensitive payloads into logs by default; log minimal useful non-sensitive context. | all logging | none stated
C R122 OL SEC-013 | DEFAULT | Security-sensitive authorization, permission and required-control checks fail safely; never silently convert a lookup or configuration failure into success. | all code | none stated
C R123 OL SEC-014 | MUST | A required security control that cannot be configured correctly fails visibly at startup/configuration time instead of falling back to an insecure mode. | all code and configuration | none stated
C R124 OL ARC-027 | MUST NOT | Physical structure follows actual responsibilities; do not create speculative projects, infrastructure, empty directory trees or generic Common/Helpers/Utils buckets solely to look architectural. | repository structure (rule loads for **/*) | none stated
